{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: \"Identifying Negative Customer Experiences Through Text Classification Using Python: A Case Study of Amazon.com Reviews on Trustpilot\"\n",
        "format: gfm\n",
        "author: \"Dominik Schulz\"\n",
        "date: today\n",
        "date-format: \"MMMM DD, YYYY\"\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "<div align=\"justify\">\n",
        "\n",
        "# Executive summary\n",
        "\n",
        "# Data\n",
        "\n",
        "The anonymized dataset `amazon_com_reviews_trustpilot_20261017_anon.feather` in the GitHub repository [dschulz13/dataset-amazon_trustpilot_reviews](https://github.com/dschulz13/dataset-amazon_trustpilot_reviews.git) is considered. It contains all **32,666 English Trustpilot reviews** for **Amazon.com** published between August 27, 2007, and October 17, 2026. The data was originally scraped directly from the [Trustpilot webpage of Amazon.com](https://de.trustpilot.com/review/www.amazon.com?languages=en) using **Python's Selenium** extension.\n",
        "\n",
        "For the sake of this project, only the available **data since January 01, 2025**, is used. This approximately gives a **cross-sectional overview of recent customer feedback**. Filtered this way, **9,522 reviews** remain for the analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        "<details><summary>Show Python code</summary>"
      ],
      "id": "cb61ef88"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: True\n",
        "\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "import matplotlib.ticker as ticker\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "\n",
        "# URL to the data on GitHub\n",
        "url = 'https://github.com/dschulz13/dataset-amazon_trustpilot_reviews/raw/refs/heads/main/amazon_com_reviews_trustpilot_20261017_anon.feather'\n",
        "\n",
        "# Read in the data\n",
        "data_filt = pd.read_feather(url)\n",
        "# Make Date column a datetime column\n",
        "data_filt['Date'] = pd.to_datetime(data_filt['Date'])\n",
        "# Keep observations since 2025-01-01 and reset index\n",
        "data_filt = (\n",
        "    data_filt[data_filt['Date'] >= '2025-01-01']\n",
        "        .reset_index(drop = True)\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Show general information\n",
        "print(data_filt.info())\n",
        "print(data_filt.describe(include = 'all'))"
      ],
      "id": "e2408722",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "</details>\n",
        "\n",
        "# Objective\n",
        "\n",
        "# Data cleaning\n",
        "\n",
        "The available review data in [dschulz13/dataset-amazon_trustpilot_reviews](https://github.com/dschulz13/dataset-amazon_trustpilot_reviews) is unformatted and raw and hence requires preliminary cleaning. Most notably, the variable `Review_Sentences` contains a list for each review entry, where each of the list's elements is a string representing an entire sentence of the review text. Consequently, the review sentences are split into individual strings in the given data. Furthermore, the review titles are available in a separate variable `Review_Title`.\n",
        "\n",
        "Initially, we only keep only reviews starting from January 01, 2025, that are thus providing some (debatable) relevance to the present day. The goal of the cleaning step is then two-fold: firstly, we wish to categorize the star rating into two groups. Those groups will be labelled `Good` and `Bad`. A good review is a review with a four- or five-start rating. A bad review covers the remaining star ratings. Of course other class constructions are also possible and could be checked in further analyses. For the prupose of this project, we, however, assume that a rating of three stars or less indicates a notable dissatisfaction with the companies' services. Secondly, we clean the text data. The following diagram highlights the individual cleaning steps. We proceed by combining the review title and the review sentences of a given observation into a single string. As the main steps, we replace emojis by specific textual representations, remove numbers and some punctutation, but not all such as `?` or `!`, remove leading and trailing whitespace, turn all letters into lower case, separate word contractions, do spelling corrections, remove stop words, and ultimately implement word stemming. This form of cleaning can be done on the entire data at the same time, as each observation is treated individually and the described procedure thus does not produce data leakage."
      ],
      "id": "428c814c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: True\n",
        "#| eval: False\n",
        "#| include: True\n",
        "\n",
        "# Standard libraries\n",
        "import re\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "\n",
        "# Special libraries\n",
        "import contractions\n",
        "from autocorrect import Speller\n",
        "import nltk\n",
        "from nltk.corpus import stopwords\n",
        "from nltk.tokenize import word_tokenize\n",
        "from nltk.stem import SnowballStemmer\n",
        "import emoji\n",
        "import gc\n",
        "from collections import Counter\n",
        "\n",
        "# Setting up spell checks and word tokenization\n",
        "nltk.download('punkt')\n",
        "nltk.download('punkt_tab')\n",
        "nltk.download('stopwords')\n",
        "stop_words = set(stopwords.words('english'))\n",
        "spell = Speller(lang = 'en')"
      ],
      "id": "b6fcdb45",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The following code chunk provides the main cleaning function for the reviews."
      ],
      "id": "678b026d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: True\n",
        "#| eval: False\n",
        "#| include: True\n",
        "\n",
        "def clean_text(dataset, spell, stop_words):\n",
        "\n",
        "    # Some format Date column as datetime and filter to use only data\n",
        "    # since the beginning of the year 2025\n",
        "    dataset['Date'] = pd.to_datetime(dataset['Date'], format = '%Y-%m-%d')\n",
        "    dataset = dataset[dataset['Date'] >= '2025-01-01 00:00:00']\n",
        "    dataset = dataset.reset_index(drop = True)\n",
        "\n",
        "    # Combine sentences into one text (and also add the review title at the beginning)\n",
        "    reviews = dataset['Review_Sentences']\n",
        "    l = len(reviews)\n",
        "    review_adj = [''] * l\n",
        "    for i in range(0, l):\n",
        "        try:\n",
        "            text0 = dataset['Review_Title'][i]\n",
        "            len_list = len(reviews[i])\n",
        "            for j in range(0, len_list):\n",
        "                text0 = text0 + ' ' + reviews[i][j]\n",
        "            review_adj[i] = text0\n",
        "        except TypeError:\n",
        "            review_adj[i] = dataset['Review_Title'][i]\n",
        "\n",
        "    dataset['Review_Sentences'] = review_adj\n",
        "\n",
        "    # Replace emojis by text replacement\n",
        "    dataset['Review_Sentences'] = [emoji.demojize(review) for review in dataset['Review_Sentences']]\n",
        "    # Replace everything except letters, numbers, whitespace, !, ?, and ' with a whitespace\n",
        "    dataset['Review_Sentences'] = dataset['Review_Sentences'].str.replace(r'[^\\w\\s\\'?!]', ' ', regex = True)\n",
        "    # Replace all numbers and multiple whitespaces in a row by a single whitespace\n",
        "    dataset['Review_Sentences'] = dataset['Review_Sentences'].str.replace(r'[\\s0-9]+', ' ', regex = True)\n",
        "    # Remove leading and trailing whitespace\n",
        "    dataset['Review_Sentences'] = dataset['Review_Sentences'].str.strip()\n",
        "    # Make everything lower case\n",
        "    dataset['Review_Sentences'] = dataset['Review_Sentences'].str.lower()\n",
        "\n",
        "    # Create a subobject of the review texts\n",
        "    reviews = dataset['Review_Sentences']\n",
        "    # Split English contractions into two words, for example \"isn't\" into \"is not\"\n",
        "    reviews = [contractions.fix(review) for review in reviews]\n",
        "    # Now replace all remaining ' by an empty string\n",
        "    reviews = [re.sub(r'\\'+', '', review) for review in reviews]\n",
        "    # Break review texts into individual words\n",
        "    reviews = [word_tokenize(review) for review in reviews]\n",
        "    # Correct spelling\n",
        "    reviews = [[spell(word) for word in review] for review in reviews]\n",
        "    # Remove stop words like \"and\"\n",
        "    reviews = [[word for word in word_list if word not in stop_words] for word_list in reviews]\n",
        "    # Stemming\n",
        "    snowball = SnowballStemmer(language = 'english')\n",
        "    reviews = [[snowball.stem(w) for w in review] for review in reviews]\n",
        "\n",
        "    l = len(reviews)\n",
        "    review_adj = [''] * l\n",
        "    for i in range(0, l):\n",
        "        len_list = len(reviews[i])\n",
        "        text0 = ''\n",
        "        for j in range(0, len_list):\n",
        "            text0 = text0 + ' ' + reviews[i][j]\n",
        "        review_adj[i] = text0\n",
        "\n",
        "    dataset['Review_Sentences'] = review_adj\n",
        "    dataset['Review_Sentences'] = dataset['Review_Sentences'].str.strip()\n",
        "    dataset['Rating_Class'] = 'Bad'\n",
        "    dataset.loc[dataset['Review_Score'] >= 4, 'Rating_Class'] = 'Good'\n",
        "\n",
        "    dataset = dataset.drop(['User_ID', 'Date', 'User_Name', 'User_Country', 'Review_Score', 'Review_Title'], axis = 1)\n",
        "\n",
        "    return dataset"
      ],
      "id": "8a0f1f62",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "After the illustrated setup, the actual data treatment can be summarized within the following few lines of code. The result is a cleaned dataset `amazon_reviews_cleaned.feather` that can be used for the actual analysis."
      ],
      "id": "75578fa3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: True\n",
        "#| include: True\n",
        "#| eval: False\n",
        "\n",
        "# Original data download from GitHub\n",
        "data_full = pd.read_feather(\"https://github.com/dschulz13/dataset-amazon_trustpilot_reviews/raw/refs/heads/main/amazon_com_reviews_trustpilot_20261017_anon.feather\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Run cleaning steps on the data\n",
        "data_full = clean_text(data_full, spell, stop_words)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Review texts are now fully cleaned and prepared for further analysis\n",
        "data_full.to_feather(f'amazon_reviews_cleaned.feather')\n",
        "\n",
        "# Delete data from memory\n",
        "del data_full\n",
        "gc.collect()"
      ],
      "id": "8c301b89",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "</div>"
      ],
      "id": "e2aa027b"
    }
